Carafe: a thread's compile buffers are sized by the block limit instead of for 65536 instructions.
Every thread that compiles takes a decoded-instruction buffer for 65536 instructions (8 MB) and two IR lists of
8 MB, 24 MB of the game's own 4 GB. A block holds at most MaxInst instructions, 1000 in Carafe, and with some 23
threads compiling at once a 32-bit game had 552 MB of its address space in these buffers and could no longer
place one 132 MB reservation. The decoder now takes MaxInst plus 64 entries, and each IR list keeps upstream's
8 MB per 5000 instructions, rounded up to whole megabytes, at least 1 MB and at most 8 MB.
MIT, as the files it changes.

